L3 gate — concurrency, throughput, and the breakdown L6 is gated on
2026-08-10 · release build · agent in the database's network namespace
· 1,000,000 JSON lines per run, batch_lines 2000
================================================================================

RESULT: 156,218 -> 491,582 lines/s, a 3.1x improvement, and the count is
still exact at maximum concurrency (rows == distinct == 1,000,000,
min 0, max 999,999).

TWO SEPARATE WINS, and the first was the surprise

  serial baseline, checkpoint per batch     156,218 lines/s
  checkpoint moved off the per-batch path   314,624 lines/s   (2.0x)
  + 8 batches in flight                     491,582 lines/s   (1.6x more)

  The first measurement of the concurrency work showed NO improvement
  at all — 156k, 155k, 155k, 154k across inflight 1/2/4/8, flat. The
  cause was a rule I had written for correctness: progress may only be
  recorded once everything in flight has landed, because batch 3 can
  finish before batch 1. True, but I was checkpointing after every
  batch, so the pipeline drained on every batch and never held more
  than one. The correctness rule was right; applying it 500 times a
  second was not.

  Moving the checkpoint onto its own interval fixed both problems at
  once: the pipeline can now actually fill, AND the agent stopped
  paying an fsync per 2,000 lines. That single change was worth more
  (2.0x) than all the concurrency (1.6x).

CONCURRENCY SWEEP

  inflight=1   3.18s   314,624 lines/s   read 1032ms   ship 3094ms (summed)
  inflight=2   2.73s   365,742 lines/s   read 1137ms   ship 4600ms
  inflight=4   2.17s   460,174 lines/s   read 1155ms   ship 3818ms
  inflight=8   1.99s   501,329 lines/s   read 1071ms   ship 3351ms

  Returns are clearly diminishing by 8 (1.09x over 4, against 1.26x
  for 4 over 2). Default stays at 4: past that the agent is buying
  little and holding more of the database's admission budget, which
  matters because TimeLakeDB admits a bounded number of concurrent
  queries and a log agent must not be why someone's dashboard is slow.

THE BREAKDOWN, AND WHAT IT SAYS ABOUT L6 (the Arrow Flight wire)

  At inflight=8: wall 1.99s, of which read/parse/encode is ~1.07s.
  The agent's own CPU is now 54% of wall clock; the wire is what
  concurrency hides. Summed across tasks, ship is ~3.2s against ~1.1s
  of read, so the wire is far more than the 10% floor the roadmap set
  as the threshold for L6 being worth starting.

  BUT the honest reading is narrower than that. `ship_ms` measures the
  whole round trip — gzip, network, server-side parse, WAL append,
  buffer apply — and Arrow Flight would only remove part of it. This
  measurement says the wire is worth investigating; it does NOT say
  how much of the wire is encoding. Separating encode from network
  from server-parse is L6's own first task, and it should happen
  before any protocol work, not after.

  What can be said with confidence: even a free wire could not exceed
  ~935k lines/s here, because 1.07s of read/encode would remain. That
  is the ceiling L6 is bidding against.

A TEST ARTEFACT THAT LOOKED LIKE DATA LOSS
  Verifying the sweep tables reported 2,000,000 and then 3,000,000 rows
  against 1,000,000 expected — while `distinct idx` was exactly
  1,000,000 every time. Not duplication: the sweep reuses table names,
  and gen.py picks a fresh time base per run, so each rerun wrote the
  same 1M indices under different timestamps and therefore different
  primary keys. The exactness run above uses a clean table. Worth
  remembering that "rows > expected with distinct == expected" means
  the corpus changed underneath, not that the agent misbehaved.

WORKSPACE
  45 tests, clippy clean, fmt clean. Shipper is now cloneable with
  shared atomic counters so batches can be in flight concurrently;
  reqwest's pool is shared across the clones.

NOT CLAIMED AT L3
  Adaptive batch sizing. The sweep shows request count is not the
  constraint at this scale (501 requests for 1M lines, ~6.5ms each),
  so sizing adaptively would add a control loop with nothing yet to
  control. Revisit if a real deployment shows batches that are too
  small under burst.
